WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.065
A private AI assistant is an application that uses models, data, interfaces,

00:00:05.065 --> 00:00:09.290
and services controlled by a person or organization under defined security and privacy

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:09.810
rules.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:13.740
It may run on a local computer, private server, isolated cloud instance,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.913
or hybrid architecture.

00:00:14.913 --> 00:00:16.821
A chatbot mainly exchanges messages.

00:00:16.821 --> 00:00:20.220
An assistant applies instructions and context to help with tasks.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.460
A knowledge assistant retrieves approved sources.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.720
An agent can select tools and perform actions,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.170
creating greater capability and greater risk.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.475
Private does not simply mean the model file is local.

00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:34.920
Prompts, documents, chat history, retrieval indexes, speech services, analytics, logs,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.890
backups, update channels, and remote access can all move or expose data.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.060
Local systems can improve control, offline capability, latency, customization,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.463
and cost predictability.

00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:48.940
Hosted services may offer stronger models, managed scaling, and less infrastructure work.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.640
Hybrid systems can route workloads according to sensitivity and capability.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.100
Control creates responsibility.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.600
The operator must size hardware, select models, maintain runtimes, secure APIs,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.430
protect data, evaluate behavior, recover failures,

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.175
and decide when the assistant should defer to a human.

00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:07.325
A successful assistant is a practical system, not a model demo.

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:11.220
It has a narrow purpose, measurable value, usable interface, grounded information,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.825
controlled access, reliable operations, and limits that users understand.

00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:16.935
Draw the trust boundary before building.

00:01:16.935 --> 00:01:21.620
Mark users, browsers, model servers, document stores, databases, tools, remote APIs,

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.220
administrators, backups, and update sources.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.140
Every crossing needs an explicit data and access decision.
